---
import Ironside from "../layouts/Ironside.astro";
import QuoteForm from "../components/QuoteForm.astro";
import CityFaq from "../components/CityFaq.astro";
import CityServiceSchema from "../components/CityServiceSchema.astro";
import ServiceAreaMap from "../components/ServiceAreaMap.astro";
import { cityBySlug, siblingsOf } from "../data/cities";

const city = cityBySlug("hamilton")!;
const nearby = siblingsOf("hamilton");

const title = "Epoxy flooring Hamilton";
const description =
 "Epoxy flooring Hamilton: Ancaster, Dundas, Stoney Creek and the Mountain. Diamond-ground prep and honest answers about tired slabs.";

const faqs = [
 {
 q: "Do you come up the escarpment, or only work the lower city?",
 a: "Both, and the escarpment is a scheduling detail rather than a barrier. The Mountain is where most of Hamilton's garages actually are - post-war and later subdivisions with attached garages and proper driveways, which are the most straightforward floors in the city. The lower city is the harder half: older housing, a lot of properties with no garage at all, and rear-lane detached garages where access is tight.",
 },
 {
 q: "My house in Westdale is from the 1930s and the garage floor is more gravel than concrete. What are my options?",
 a: "That is a common find in Westdale, Kirkendall and the Durand. Some of those garages were never poured with a proper slab - you have a thin skim over compacted fill, or a slab that has broken into pieces. A coating needs a sound substrate, so this becomes a pour-a-new-slab conversation before it is a coating conversation. We will say so at the quote rather than take the job. Coating a failing slab hides the problem for one season and then telegraphs every crack.",
 },
 {
 q: "Is industrial fallout from the north end an issue for a coating?",
 a: "For the coating itself, no - a cured polyaspartic or epoxy surface is chemically robust and washes down. It matters during install. Airborne dust settling into a wet topcoat is a genuine risk on a windy day close to the industrial waterfront, so on those jobs we keep the bay door managed and time the topcoat rather than leaving the garage open to the street. It is one of the few places where we will adjust the working method for the neighbourhood.",
 },
 {
 q: "Do you cover Ancaster, Dundas and Stoney Creek, or are those separate?",
 a: "They are part of the City of Hamilton and we treat them as one service area. In practice they are three different jobs. Ancaster and Dundas have a lot of older village-core properties with detached garages and awkward access, plus newer estate subdivisions with large double and triple garages. Stoney Creek is mostly newer subdivision stock on the Mountain side with flat, predictable slabs. Waterdown and Binbrook are in range too.",
 },
 {
 q: "Hamilton winters seem harder than Toronto's. Does that affect the floor?",
 a: "The freeze-thaw cycling on the Mountain is genuinely harsher than lakeside Toronto - you get more crossings of the freezing point in a season, and every crossing works water in and out of the concrete surface. That is an argument for coating rather than against it: a bonded coating stops water entering the surface at all. It is also an argument for doing the prep properly, because a coating that is bonded to a weak, already-scaled surface will bring that surface off with it.",
 },
 {
 q: "Can you coat a shop or industrial unit in the north end?",
 a: "Yes, and it is a good fit for Hamilton's stock of small industrial bays. Those floors want a different specification than a residential garage - heavier build, often a broadcast quartz rather than decorative flake, and line marking if it is a working floor. Scheduling is usually the constraint, since we need the bay cleared and the forklift traffic stopped for the cure window. Weekend installs are normal for these.",
 },
 {
 q: "How far ahead do I need to book?",
 a: "It varies with the season rather than the city - spring and early autumn are the busy stretches everywhere in the region. Hamilton has one advantage: it is a thirty-minute run down the QEW from our Oakville shop, so it slots into the schedule more easily than the north end of the GTA. Winter is genuinely available here because polyaspartic cures in cold, and an unheated Hamilton garage in January is a workable job in a way that it would not be for standard epoxy.",
 },
];
---

<Ironside title={title} description={description} heroType="dark">
 <CityServiceSchema
 city={city.name}
 citySlug={city.slug}
 wikidata={city.wikidata}
 description={description}
 />

 <section class="hero-dark compact">
 <div class="hero-grad"></div><div class="hero-grain"></div><div class="hero-rim"></div>
 <div class="hero-inner">
 <div class="city-hero-grid">
 <div>
 <div class="kicker mb-2">Epoxy &amp; polyaspartic &middot; Hamilton</div>
 <h1 class="h-hero" style="margin-top:12px;max-width:16ch">Epoxy flooring in Hamilton.</h1>
 <p class="lede" style="color:var(--color-iron-300);margin-top:24px;max-width:56ch">
 Above the escarpment and below it - Ancaster, Dundas, Stoney Creek, Westdale and the
 Mountain. Diamond-ground prep, honest answers about slabs that are not worth coating,
 and a floor you can park on the next morning.
 </p>
 <div style="display:flex;gap:12px;margin-top:36px;flex-wrap:wrap">
 <a href="#quote" class="btn btn-lg btn-primary">Book a slab assessment</a>
 </div>
 <div class="meta-rule" style="margin-top:36px">
 <span>{city.driveMinutes} min from the Oakville shop</span><span class="sep"></span>
 <span>Residential, commercial and small industrial</span>
 </div>
 </div>
 <div id="quote">
 <QuoteForm city={city.name} heading="Free quote in Hamilton" sub="We measure the slab, test it for moisture, and quote a fixed price." />
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>

 <section class="section">
 <div class="container">
 <div class="split-64" style="align-items:end">
 <div>
 <div class="kicker">Hamilton slabs</div>
 <h2 class="h1 mt-2" style="max-width:22ch">The escarpment splits Hamilton garage floors into two jobs</h2>
 </div>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);max-width:52ch">
 Almost everything about a Hamilton garage floor follows from whether it sits above or below
 the escarpment. Age of housing, whether there is a garage at all, and how we get equipment
 to it all change at that line.
 </p>
 </div>

 <div class="hood-grid">
 <div class="hood">
 <h4>Hamilton Mountain</h4>
 <p>Post-war through modern subdivisions, attached garages, driveways to stage from. The most straightforward floors in the city and the bulk of what we coat here.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="hood">
 <h4>Westdale &amp; the Durand</h4>
 <p>1920s-30s stock. Detached rear garages where they exist at all, often with a thin or failing slab. This is the area where we most often recommend a new pour instead of a coating.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="hood">
 <h4>Ancaster</h4>
 <p>Two markets in one: older village-core properties with tight access, and newer estate subdivisions with large double and triple garages and high finish expectations.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="hood">
 <h4>Dundas</h4>
 <p>Valley-town housing, a lot of it older, with narrow lots and constrained driveways. Charming to look at, fiddly to run a grinder into.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="hood">
 <h4>Stoney Creek &amp; Binbrook</h4>
 <p>Mostly newer subdivision stock with flat, well-cured slabs. Predictable, quick, and a good candidate for a same-day polyaspartic install.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>

 <section class="section section-sm" style="background:var(--color-iron-50);border-top:1px solid var(--color-iron-100);border-bottom:1px solid var(--color-iron-100)">
 <div class="container">
 <div class="split-64" style="align-items:start;gap:56px">
 <div>
 <div class="kicker">Access and conditions</div>
 <h2 class="h2 mt-2" style="max-width:24ch">Hamilton epoxy flooring access: steep drives, tight lanes, salt</h2>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:20px;max-width:60ch">
 Hamilton throws two access problems at us that the rest of the region does not. The first
 is gradient - a good number of Mountain-edge and Dundas properties have driveways steep
 enough that we stage equipment at the top and run hose down, which adds time to the front
 of the day. The second is the lower city's rear lanes, which are narrower than Toronto's
 and often shared, so we need to know in advance whether the truck can actually reach the
 door.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px;max-width:60ch">
 Then there is the salt. The Mountain sees more freeze-thaw crossings per winter than the
 lakeshore does, and the roads are treated accordingly. On an uncoated slab that shows up
 as scaling - the surface paste breaking away in flakes, exposing aggregate. A scaled slab
 is still coatable, but the grind has to go past the damaged layer to sound concrete, and
 that is more work than a clean floor. We would rather price that honestly than discover it
 on install day.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px;max-width:60ch">
 The upside of the Hamilton market is that it is genuinely underserved. There are fewer
 dedicated coating contractors working here than in the Toronto or Peel markets, which
 means shorter lead times and less of the quote-and-vanish behaviour homeowners complain
 about. We are thirty minutes down the QEW and we will come and look at the floor.
 </p>
 </div>
 <div>
 <ServiceAreaMap city={city.name} lat={city.lat} lng={city.lng} span={0.15} />
 <div style="margin-top:24px">
 <div class="kicker">Areas we cover</div>
 <div class="nearby">
 {city.neighbourhoods.map((n) => <a href="#quote">{n}</a>)}
 <a href="#quote">Waterdown</a>
 <a href="#quote">Binbrook</a>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>

 <section class="section">
 <div class="container-narrow">
 <div class="kicker">When we say no</div>
 <h2 class="h2 mt-2">Some Hamilton garage floors should not be coated at all</h2>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:20px">
 This comes up more here than anywhere else we work, because of the age of the lower-city
 housing stock. A coating is a surface treatment. It is only ever as good as the concrete it
 is bonded to. If the slab underneath has broken into plates, heaved, or was never properly
 poured in the first place, a coating will look excellent for one season and then map every
 defect straight through.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 The tells we look for are a step of more than roughly 10&nbsp;mm across a crack, active
 moisture with no obvious source, widespread scaling deeper than a few millimetres, and a slab
 that sounds hollow when tapped. Any of those and the honest recommendation is repair or
 replacement first. It is a shorter conversation than a warranty claim two years later.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 Where the slab is sound, the process is the same as everywhere: diamond grind to profile,
 chase and fill the cracks, moisture test, coat. No acid etching. In a city with this much
 salt exposure, a properly bonded coating is one of the more genuinely useful things you can
 do to a garage.
 </p>
 <div class="nearby">
 <a href="/polyaspartic-garage-floors">How polyaspartic differs from epoxy</a>
 <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-cost">What a floor costs</a>
 <a href="/epoxy-flooring">All service areas</a>
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>


 <section class="section section-sm" style="border-top:1px solid var(--color-iron-100)">
 <div class="container-narrow">
 <div class="kicker">Working through the seasons</div>
 <h2 class="h2 mt-2">Freeze-thaw is why Hamilton garage floor coating pays off</h2>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:20px">
 Hamilton sits at an awkward elevation for concrete. The Mountain is high enough and far
 enough from the lake's moderating effect that it crosses the freezing point more often across
 a winter than lakeside Oakville or Toronto do. Every crossing is a cycle: water in the
 surface pores expands as it freezes, forces the paste apart fractionally, then thaws and
 soaks in a little deeper.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 Repeat that a few hundred times with road salt in the mix and you get scaling - the smooth
 cement paste at the surface breaking away in flakes and exposing the aggregate underneath. It
 is the defining condition of an uncoated Hamilton garage floor and it is why so many slabs
 here look rougher than their age suggests.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 For a coating this cuts both ways. A bonded, chloride-resistant surface stops the cycle
 entirely, which makes the argument for doing it stronger here than in a milder microclimate.
 But a scaled slab is a harder slab to prepare, because the grind has to go past the damaged
 layer to reach sound concrete. If we quote a Mountain floor higher than a comparable
 Stoney Creek one, that is usually why.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 Install season is effectively year-round with polyaspartic, and winter is often the better
 time in Hamilton - the garage is empty of garden equipment, the slab is not sweating, and our
 schedule has more room. The one condition we will not work in is an actively wet slab, which
 in the lower city usually means waiting for a thaw to finish rather than for a date.
 </p>
 </div>
 </section>

 <section class="section section-sm" style="background:var(--color-iron-50);border-top:1px solid var(--color-iron-100);border-bottom:1px solid var(--color-iron-100)">
 <div class="container-narrow">
 <div class="kicker">Before you sign anything</div>
 <h2 class="h2 mt-2">Reading an epoxy flooring quote on an older Hamilton slab</h2>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:20px">
 Hamilton has the oldest average housing stock in our service area, and old slabs are where
 quotes diverge most. Two contractors can look at the same 1940s Westdale garage and price it
 forty per cent apart, and the gap is almost always about how much repair is included versus
 assumed away.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 <strong>Is slab repair in the number, or excluded?</strong> A quote that assumes a sound
 substrate on a seventy-year-old floor is a quote that will be revised upward on install day.
 Ask for the repair scope in writing.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 <strong>How deep is the grind?</strong> On a scaled floor, "surface preparation" can mean a
 light pass that leaves the damaged layer in place. The coating will then bond beautifully to
 concrete that is already letting go.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 <strong>Will they tell you not to do it?</strong> This is the real test. Some Hamilton slabs
 should be replaced, not coated. A contractor who has never turned down a floor is not
 assessing them.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 The city is genuinely underserved for this trade compared with Toronto or Peel, which means
 less competition on price but also fewer specialists. It is worth asking how many coatings
 someone has actually installed, as opposed to how many years they have been in construction.
 </p>
 </div>
 </section>


 <section class="section section-sm">
 <div class="container-narrow">
 <div class="kicker">Install day</div>
 <h2 class="h2 mt-2">How a Hamilton epoxy floor install actually runs</h2>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:20px">
 Thirty minutes down the QEW means an 8am start without difficulty. On steep Mountain-edge or
 Dundas properties the first task is working out where the truck sits, because the grinder and
 vacuum run off it and the hose reach is finite. On a very steep drive we stage at the top and
 run down, which adds twenty minutes to setup and is worth doing properly.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 Then the grind, and in Hamilton this is the stage that most often changes the plan. A scaled
 slab looks superficially fine until the grinder takes the weak paste off and reveals how deep
 the damage goes. Where that happens we go deeper to reach sound concrete rather than coating
 over a surface that is already releasing. On the rare occasion the slab turns out to be
 unsound throughout, we stop and have an honest conversation instead of proceeding.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 Assuming the slab is good: vacuum, inspect, basecoat, flake to refusal, scrape-back, topcoat
 mid-afternoon. Walkable the same evening, car back the following morning. Industrial bays run
 differently - heavier build, often over a weekend, and line marking last.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 Identical process to the one we run in <a href="/epoxy-flooring-milton">Milton</a> and
 <a href="/epoxy-flooring-mississauga">Mississauga</a>. More on
 <a href="/about">how we work</a>, and the full
 <a href="/service-areas">service-area map</a>.
 </p>
 </div>
 </section>

 <CityFaq faqs={faqs} city={city.name} />

 <section class="section section-sm">
 <div class="container">
 <div class="kicker">Nearby</div>
 <h2 class="h3 mt-2">Epoxy flooring in nearby cities</h2>
 <div class="nearby">
 <a href="/service-areas/oakville">Oakville</a>
 {nearby.map((c) => <a href={`/epoxy-flooring-${c.slug}`}>{c.name}</a>)}
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>
</Ironside>
